142	<section id="about" class="part">
143	<h2>What this page is</h2>
144	<p>The <code>fortress-repo</code> skill holds the rules that bind every worker in the repository. This page shows you each change that landed in it, as a diff with its reason, so that you can check what the workers are now told whenever you want. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat.</p>
145	</section>
146	
147	<section id="s1" class="part">
148	<h2>Not yet read</h2>
149	<p>Eleven items, none of them read. The page holds only what you have not read. At 07:45 UTC on 8 November, your comment on change 38 marked changes 37 and 38 read, so they left, and the page now starts at change 39. No comment marks a later item read.</p>
150	<p>Climb batch 27 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 16:18 and 16:28 UTC on 7 November. Changes 39 to 41 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the update rewrote three of its sections for the landing, its judge said to install all three, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 42 to 47 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk, the library and the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
151	<p>Change 48 came after the landing, at your word in chat. The skill writer made it in two commits to <code>committing.md</code>, at 16:42 and 16:48 UTC on 7 November, the second with the fixes after its cold read.</p>
152	<p>On 8 November, your comments on changes 37 and 39 led to two more commits. At your word of 01:07 UTC, the skill writer made the fixes in one commit at 01:09 UTC. A cold read then found passages that would send a worker wrong, and the skill writer fixed them in a second commit at 02:06 UTC. The two commits changed three passages. One is the passage of change 39, which now shows these changes too, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November&rdquo;. Another, in <code>SKILL.md</code>, is change 49. The third is the passage of change 37, which you have since read.</p>
153	<p>Five items are new rules: change 39, a type without its own <code>=</code> compares by strict equivalence; change 40, the static argument written where walk's printed type misleads; change 41, <code>Number</code> declares nothing; change 42, walk's float numerals under strict equivalence; change 48, a commit already made whose footer names a model is left as it is. The other five from the batch state facts that it changed, and change no rule. Change 49 is not a new rule either: it defines two terms. Nor are the changes of 8 November to change 39: it now spells out strict equivalence and three cases where a worker would guess wrong.</p>
154	<p>Each item shows the skill&rsquo;s current text, as it stands on <code>main</code>, against the text before the item&rsquo;s first commit. When a later commit changes an item&rsquo;s passage, the change is folded into that item, with its own reason, and does not become a new item. The words that came in are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. The next new change will be change 50.</p>
155	
156	<section id="l39" class="part item">
157	<h3><span class="num">Change 39 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>A type without its own = compares by strict equivalence</h3>
158	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
159	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, lines 55 to 62, a new rule after the rule on pairs and triples</p>
160	<p><span class="add"><strong>Where a type declares no <code>=</code>, <code>=</code> is strict equivalence, <code>SEQV</code> (P-168).</strong> Strict equivalence is the operator <code>&#x2263;</code> (STRICTLY EQUIVALENT TO), written <code>SEQV</code> or <code>===</code> in ASCII. The operator <code>&#x2261;</code> (IDENTICAL TO, <code>EQV</code> or <code>EQUIV</code>) is a different one: logical equivalence on booleans. The interpreter's library declares it for no type, and walk stops on it (row 184): write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> answers <code>a SEQV b</code>. <code>SEQV</code> follows the specification on both paths:</span></p>
161	<ul>
162	<li><span class="add">values of different types are never equivalent;</span></li>
163	<li><span class="add">a value object is equivalent field by field, a tuple element by element, an integer, a character or a boolean by its value, and a float by its bits;</span></li>
164	<li><span class="add">a reference object, a string among them, is equivalent only to itself;</span></li>
165	<li><span class="add">a function is equivalent to itself, but may be found unequal to another that behaves the same.</span></li>
166	</ul>
167	<p><span class="add">So for a value object <code>V</code> without its own <code>=</code>, <code>V(s) = V(t)</code> is <code>false</code> where <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> are equal strings made by two computations. Under walk, each evaluation of a string literal makes a new string, unlike Java, which interns literals. So <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> and <code>V("ab") = V("ab")</code> are <code>false</code>. The library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so <code>(s, 1, 2, 3) = (t, 1, 2, 3)</code> is <code>false</code> too. Both <code>false</code> answers are the rule's: assert them as correct. Under walk a float numeral is a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, so it is not equivalent to a computed <code>RR64</code> (<code>interpreter.md</code>, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;). To compare by content, give the value object an <code>=</code>, or compare the parts. A string's <code>=</code> compares text. Under walk, <code>&#x2260;</code> (<code>=/=</code> or <code>NE</code>) and <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> reach a type's own <code>=</code> only if the type extends a library trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code> (row 807). Otherwise write <code>assert(x = y, msg)</code>. Until the switch-over, the compiler's library declares no <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> and none on tuples. Its own <code>&#x2263;</code>, declared as <code>===</code>, compares strings and <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code> by <code>=</code>, not by bits or identity (row 908).</span></p>
168	</div></div>
169	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. The batch made both paths answer <code>SEQV</code>, strict equivalence, by the specification's rule, as your decision on its meaning asks. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code>, which every type without its own <code>=</code> falls back to, answers <code>SEQV</code>. The old part said nothing of this. A worker would expect such an <code>=</code> to compare content, and would take the two <code>false</code> answers in the new text for defects. The new rule says how <code>SEQV</code> compares, gives those two cases and says to assert them as correct, and gives the fix. It also names walk's limit on <code>assert</code>, and what the compiler's library does until the switch-over. After the cold read, it says how an integer, a character or a boolean compares: by its value. It names a string's kind as &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo;, in place of &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;, which the skill does not define. It writes the two <code>false</code> cases as examples, in place of a string &ldquo;built apart&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply. It points to walk's float numerals (change 42). And it says what <code>===</code> is: the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>.</p>
170	<p><strong>Why, 8 November.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of the rule's meaning: what <code>SEQV</code> answers stays as the batch made it. The text now spells the operators out, and it adds three cases where a worker would guess wrong. Your comments on this change raised most of it. At 00:35 UTC on 8 November you asked to check the Unicode spelling of <code>SEQV</code> and <code>=/=</code>, and at 00:58 you asked that the text give both spellings and tell &ldquo;strictly equivalent to&rdquo; from &ldquo;identical to&rdquo;. The batch's text called <code>===</code> &ldquo;the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>&rdquo;, but <code>SEQV</code> is an ASCII spelling too, and the text never showed the characters. It now gives all three spellings of strict equivalence, <code>&#x2263;</code>, <code>SEQV</code> and <code>===</code>; it says that <code>&#x2261;</code> is another operator; and it says that <code>=/=</code> is <code>&#x2260;</code>. At 00:44 you asked that the skill use &ldquo;reference object&rdquo;, the term of the specification and of peer languages such as Swift. The batch's cold read had put &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo; in its place, since the skill did not define the term. Change 49 now defines it, so the bullet on strings says &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;. A first wording of the fix called <code>&#x2261;</code> &ldquo;if and only if&rdquo; on booleans. The cold read ran it under walk, and walk stops on it, since the interpreter's library declares <code>&#x2261;</code> for no type. The text now calls it logical equivalence, the specification's words, and says to write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The cold read also found two cases that the text left to a guess. Under walk, two equal string literals are two strings, so <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> is <code>false</code>, where a worker who knows Java expects one shared string. And once change 49 took functions out of the reference objects, no bullet said what <code>SEQV</code> gives on two functions. A new bullet says it, from the specification.</p></div>
171	</section>
172	
173	<section id="l40" class="part item">
174	<h3><span class="num">Change 40 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Walk prints ZZ32 where the instance is Int</h3>
175	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
176	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, line 89, the paragraph &ldquo;Under walk a numeral has a run-time class&rdquo;, up to its sentence on walk's error messages; the sentences after it did not change</p>
177	<p><strong>Under walk a numeral has a run-time class.</strong> The checker types an integer numeral as <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling under <code>Number</code> that converts to the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> (P-76). Walk gives a numeral the class <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> or <code>BigNum</code> by its value. These classes stand for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, but a generic instantiates at the class<span class="cut">: <code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code></span><span class="add">. Walk prints these classes as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside static arguments, so a printed type does not show the instance: <code>Cell(3)</code> prints <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> but is a <code>Cell[\Int\]</code>, and no <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code></span> (row 364). <span class="add">Where you need a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, write <code>Cell[\ZZ32\](3)</code>. Walk's own error messages say <code>Int</code>.</span></p>
178	</div></div>
179	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule, beside a fact that changed. The batch changed how walk prints a type: it now prints its numeral classes <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code> and <code>BigNum</code> as <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code>, also inside a static argument. The old example, &ldquo;<code>Box[\Int\]</code> is not <code>Box[\ZZ32\]</code>&rdquo;, named a type that walk no longer prints. A worker who saw <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> in walk's output would take the value for a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code>, and a dispatch on that type would then fail. The text now says that the printed name hides the instance. After the cold read, it says what to write where a <code>Cell[\ZZ32\]</code> is needed, the workaround that ledger row 364 records. It names the printed names outright, in place of &ldquo;the traits' names&rdquo;. And the sentence on walk's error messages lost a &ldquo;still&rdquo;, which told of a change rather than of what a worker sees.</p></div>
180	</section>
181	
182	<section id="l41" class="part item">
183	<h3><span class="num">Change 41 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Number declares nothing</h3>
184	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
185	<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, line 93, a new rule after the rule on coercions</p>
186	<p><span class="add"><strong><code>Number</code> declares nothing (P-165).</strong> Each of the eight number types, and <code>IntLiteral</code>, declares its own <code>asFloat</code> into <code>RR64</code>. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call on a <code>T</code> no method or operator that the number types declare, <code>asFloat</code> among them. Two numbers of different types compare by <code>=</code> only where a coercion carries one into the other: the library declares <code>=</code> for each such pair, so a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value are <code>=</code>. Otherwise <code>=</code> is <code>SEQV</code> (&ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;), and answers <code>false</code> without an error: <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> is <code>false</code>, as is a <code>ZZ64</code> against an equal <code>RR64</code>. To compare values across types at known types, write <code>asFloat(x) = y</code>. In generic code over <code>T extends Number</code>, declare your function once for each number type instead. A program may also declare its own number type below <code>Number</code> (P-160).</span></p>
187	</div></div>
188	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. By your decision, <code>asFloat</code>, and the <code>=</code> built on it, left the trait <code>Number</code>, and the batch made it so. Each of the eight number types, and <code>IntLiteral</code>, now declares its own <code>asFloat</code>, and <code>Number</code> declares nothing. So generic code over <code>T extends Number</code> can call nothing on a <code>T</code> that the number types declare. Two numbers of different types are <code>=</code> only where a coercion joins them. Otherwise <code>=</code> falls back to <code>SEQV</code> and answers <code>false</code>, with no error. The old part said none of this: a worker would have called <code>asFloat</code> on a <code>T</code>, or expected <code>1/2 = 0.5</code> to be <code>true</code>. After the cold read, the text gives a pair that a coercion joins, a <code>ZZ32</code> and a <code>ZZ64</code> of equal value, which are <code>=</code>. It says &ldquo;declare your function once for each number type&rdquo;, since &ldquo;the function&rdquo; could be read as <code>asFloat</code>. And its last sentence no longer says why a program may declare its own number type: change 37 now gives that reason.</p></div>
189	</section>
