213	<ul><li>Under walk, <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> checks that <code>x</code> equals <code>y</code>. It and <code>=/=</code> call a type's own <code>=</code> only if the type extends a <code>FortressLibrary</code> trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code><span class="add"> (ledger row 807)</span>. Otherwise they compare by <span class="cut">identity (ledger row 807)</span><span class="add"><code>SEQV</code> (<code>revival-changes.md</code>, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;)</span>. If a type <code>P</code> has its own <code>=</code>, make <code>P</code> extend <code>Equality[\P\]</code>, or write <code>assert(x = y, msg)</code>. The compiler's library declares the two-value <code>assert</code> only for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>String</code> and <code>Character</code>; its <code>assert(flag, msg)</code> takes any <code>Boolean</code>.</li></ul>
