215	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the advice stays, and the fact under it changed. Under walk, <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> and <code>=/=</code> skip a type's own <code>=</code> unless the type extends a library trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code>. The bullet said that they then compare by identity. Since the batch, they fall back to <code>SEQV</code>, which compares a value object field by field. The first wording kept the ledger row's citation at the end of the clause on <code>SEQV</code>, so a worker could read <code>SEQV</code>'s answer as the defect and write a test that asserts the opposite. After the cold read, the citation stands on what the row records: the skipped <code>=</code>. The bullet no longer restates <code>SEQV</code> in short. It points to the rule in <code>revival-changes.md</code> (change 39).</p></div>
