263	<h3><span class="num">Change 49 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;Parallelism and mutable state&rdquo;</span>Value objects and reference objects defined</h3>
264	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
265	<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;Parallelism and mutable state&rdquo;, line 112, the paragraph on immutable and mutable variables</p>
266	<p><code>x = e</code> declares an immutable variable. <code>var x: T = e</code> and <code>x: T := e</code> declare a mutable one, and <code>x := e</code> assigns to it (<code>Specification/basic/variables.tex</code>). These are Scala's <code>val</code> and <code>var</code>, or Swift's <code>let</code> and <code>var</code>. A field is immutable unless it is declared with <code>var</code>. A <span class="cut"><code>value</code></span> <span class="add">value object is a tuple, <code>()</code>, or an</span> object <span class="add">whose declaration, or a trait that it extends, has the <code>value</code> modifier, as the library's numbers, characters and booleans do. It</span> has only immutable fields and value semantics, like a Swift <code>struct</code>. <span class="add">Any other object, except a function, is a reference object, like an instance of a Swift class. Each construction makes a new reference object, distinct from all others (<code>Specification/basic/objects.tex:24-33</code>).</span></p>
267	</div></div>
268	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the skill now defines two terms that its rules use. Your comment on change 39, at 00:44 UTC on 8 November, raised it: &ldquo;reference object&rdquo; is the term of peer languages such as Swift, so the skill should define it and use it. The specification uses the term too, and defines it. The old sentence said what a value object has, but not which objects are value objects, and it gave the other objects no name. So change 39 had to call a string &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo;. The text now defines both terms from the specification: a value object is a tuple, <code>()</code>, or an object whose declaration, or a trait that it extends, has the <code>value</code> modifier; any other object, except a function, is a reference object, and each construction makes a new one. The first wording said only &ldquo;an object of a type declared with the <code>value</code> modifier&rdquo;. The cold read found that this made a tuple and <code>()</code> reference objects, where the specification makes them value objects and walk compares a tuple element by element. It also found that the wording did not say which of an object's types counts: an integer is of the trait <code>ZZ32</code>, which is not declared <code>value</code>, and of the object <code>Int</code>, which is. After the cold read, the text names tuples and <code>()</code>, and puts the modifier on the object's declaration or on a trait that it extends, as for the library's numbers, characters and booleans.</p></div>
