<ul><li>Under walk, <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> checks that <code>x</code> equals <code>y</code>. It and <code>=/=</code> call a type's own <code>=</code> only if the type extends a <code>FortressLibrary</code> trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code><span class="add"> (ledger row 807)</span>. Otherwise they compare by <span class="cut">identity (ledger row 807)</span><span class="add"><code>SEQV</code> (<code>revival-changes.md</code>, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;)</span>. If a type <code>P</code> has its own <code>=</code>, make <code>P</code> extend <code>Equality[\P\]</code>, or write <code>assert(x = y, msg)</code>. The compiler's library declares the two-value <code>assert</code> only for <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>String</code> and <code>Character</code>; its <code>assert(flag, msg)</code> takes any <code>Boolean</code>.</li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the advice stays, and the fact under it changed. Under walk, <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> and <code>=/=</code> skip a type's own <code>=</code> unless the type extends a library trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code>. The bullet said that they then compare by identity. Since the batch, they fall back to <code>SEQV</code>, which compares a value object field by field. The first wording kept the ledger row's citation at the end of the clause on <code>SEQV</code>, so a worker could read <code>SEQV</code>'s answer as the defect and write a test that asserts the opposite. After the cold read, the citation stands on what the row records: the skipped <code>=</code>. The bullet no longer restates <code>SEQV</code> in short. It points to the rule in <code>revival-changes.md</code> (change 39).</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l45" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 45 &middot; compiler.md, &ldquo;What the compiled path cannot compile yet&rdquo;</span>The code generator compiles a type assumption, but for three forms</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
<p class="where-in">compiler.md, &ldquo;What the compiled path cannot compile yet&rdquo;, lines 50 to 54, a new paragraph and list after the forms of local function that the code generator refuses</p>
<p><span class="add">A type assumption <code>e asif T</code> has the value of <code>e</code>, but the call around it chooses its declaration as if <code>e</code> were a <code>T</code>, a supertype of <code>e</code>'s type (<code>Specification/basic/expressions/type-annotation.tex</code>). The code generator compiles one at a dotted call's receiver, at a call of a functional method or an overloaded function whose every argument is an assumption, and elsewhere as the value of <code>e</code>. It refuses these forms (ledger row 818):</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span class="add">a call of a functional method or an overloaded function with an assumption at some of its arguments but not all: &lsquo;Can't compile a dispatched call whose arguments are not all type assumptions&rsquo;;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a generic method on an assumed receiver: &lsquo;Can't compile a type assumption at the receiver of a generic method yet&rsquo;;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a call of an overloaded generic function with an assumption at an argument: &lsquo;Can't compile a type assumption at a call of an overloaded generic function yet&rsquo;.</span></li>
</ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the code generator learned a new form. A type assumption, <code>e asif T</code>, keeps the value of <code>e</code>, but makes the call around it choose its declaration as if <code>e</code> were of the supertype <code>T</code>. The batch taught the code generator to compile one at a dotted call's receiver, at a call whose arguments are all assumptions, and anywhere else as the plain value. It refuses three forms, each with its own message. The part said nothing of type assumptions. A worker would have kept them out of compiled code, or recorded a refusal as some other failure. After the cold read, the part defines a type assumption from the specification. It names the calls that need an assumption at every argument or at none: a call of a functional method or of an overloaded function. The first wording said &ldquo;every dispatched argument&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply, since a Fortress call dispatches on every argument. And each refused form is named by the call that holds it.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l46" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 46 &middot; compiler.md, &ldquo;What the code generator does&rdquo;</span>The value modifier marks the compiled class</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
<p class="where-in">compiler.md, &ldquo;What the code generator does&rdquo;, line 68, its third bullet</p>
