192	<h3><span class="num">Change 42 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;</span>Walk's float numeral is not equivalent to a computed RR64</h3>
193	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
194	<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;, line 18, a new paragraph after the one on how walk chooses a coercion</p>
195	<p><span class="add">Walk keeps a float numeral as a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, also where it is bound to an <code>RR64</code> variable, parameter or field. So <code>SEQV</code> finds it unequal to a computed <code>RR64</code> of the same bits. This <code>false</code> follows the rule that values of different types are never equivalent, and no ledger row holds it (F-326). For <code>value object V(f: RR64)</code> with no <code>=</code> of its own, <code>x: RR64 = 0.25 + 0.25</code> and <code>y: RR64 = 0.5</code>, <code>V(0.5) = V(x)</code> is <code>false</code>. So is <code>(y, 1, 2, 3) = (x, 1, 2, 3)</code>, since the library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only. Give the value object its own <code>=</code> and make it extend <code>Equality[\V\]</code>, or compare the parts.</span></p>
196	</div></div>
197	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule: a trap and its fix. Walk keeps a float numeral, such as <code>0.5</code>, as a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, also where it binds to an <code>RR64</code>. By the <code>SEQV</code> rule (change 39), values of different types are never equivalent. So a value object without its own <code>=</code> that holds such a numeral is not <code>=</code> to one that holds a computed <code>RR64</code> of the same bits, and a tuple of four or more is not either. The orientation update's judge ran this under walk and found it. The new <code>SEQV</code> rule alone would lead a worker to expect <code>true</code>, and the judge sent the point to this part. After the cold read, the text says that the <code>false</code> is the rule's answer and that no ledger row holds it, so that a worker knows not to record it as a defect. It says that the numeral stays a <code>FloatLiteral</code> at a variable, a parameter or a field. It writes the tuple case as an example, with its reason: the library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only. And the fix adds <code>Equality[\V\]</code>, since without it walk's two-value <code>assert</code> skips the type's own <code>=</code> and still answers <code>false</code>.</p></div>
198	</section>
