229	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule's meaning: the code generator learned a new form. A type assumption, <code>e asif T</code>, keeps the value of <code>e</code>, but makes the call around it choose its declaration as if <code>e</code> were of the supertype <code>T</code>. The batch taught the code generator to compile one at a dotted call's receiver, at a call whose arguments are all assumptions, and anywhere else as the plain value. It refuses three forms, each with its own message. The part said nothing of type assumptions. A worker would have kept them out of compiled code, or recorded a refusal as some other failure. After the cold read, the part defines a type assumption from the specification. It names the calls that need an assumption at every argument or at none: a call of a functional method or of an overloaded function. The first wording said &ldquo;every dispatched argument&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply, since a Fortress call dispatches on every argument. And each refused form is named by the call that holds it.</p></div>
