259	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. You do not care whether a commit&rsquo;s footer names a model. In chat at 05:17 and 05:19 UTC on 7 November, you said that you would prefer the footer to name the model, but that tracking which model did which work would be messy. At 14:19 UTC you said: &ldquo;record in the skills, whichever needs it, that I don&rsquo;t care&rdquo;. The skill gave the plain footer, but said nothing of a commit whose footer already names a model, and its rule in &ldquo;What to commit&rdquo; forbids a model&rsquo;s identifier in a commit message. So a worker could take such a footer for a defect, and check for it, report it or list it. The new sentences keep the plain footer as what an agent writes. A commit already made whose footer names a model is left as it is, with no check, report or list of it. The first wording said only &ldquo;If a commit&rsquo;s footer names a model&rdquo;. After the cold read, it speaks of a commit already made, so that a worker still writes the plain footer, and it covers a model named by any name or identifier, so that the rule on identifiers does not make such a footer a defect.</p></div>
